gcc: search <sysroot>/include/fortify ahead of the musl system headers

Wire the vendored fortify-headers (../fortify-headers/, installed into
<sysroot>/include/fortify/ by the top-level Makefile) into gcc's musl include
defaults, so `-D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=N` resolves the fortified wrappers (which
#include_next the real musl headers). Bare musl provides no fortify machinery,
so without this the flag is a silent no-op.

NB: Alpine adds this to INCLUDE_DEFAULTS_MUSL_NATIVE, which works for their
*native* gcc (musl headers at NATIVE_SYSTEM_HEADER_DIR = /usr/include). This is
a *cross* toolchain: musl is found via TOOL_INCLUDE_DIR ($prefix/<triple>/
include), which INCLUDE_DEFAULTS searches *before* MUSL_NATIVE — so a fortify
entry in MUSL_NATIVE lands after musl and never wins. Put it in MUSL_TOOL,
immediately before the TOOL_INCLUDE_DIR entry, so <sysroot>/include/fortify is
searched just ahead of <sysroot>/include. Prefix-relative (add_sysroot=0),
matching the sibling TOOL entry; cxx_aware=1 (the headers guard __cplusplus).

--- a/gcc/config/linux.h
+++ b/gcc/config/linux.h
@@ -152,6 +152,7 @@
 
 #ifdef TOOL_INCLUDE_DIR
 #define INCLUDE_DEFAULTS_MUSL_TOOL			\
+    { TOOL_INCLUDE_DIR "/fortify", "BINUTILS", 0, 1, 0, 0},	\
     { TOOL_INCLUDE_DIR, "BINUTILS", 0, 1, 0, 0},
 #else
 #define INCLUDE_DEFAULTS_MUSL_TOOL
